# Level 3-03: Two Gates — two separate AND-doors gate different plate subsets on the same
# path to the goal: door A (5,6) needs plates 1+2, door B (5,10) needs plate 3 alone. Plate 3's
# branch (right at y=8) sits *past* door A, so whichever ghost is sent for plate 3 must be able
# to walk through door A while recording — which means plates 1 and 2 must already be held by
# ghosts that already exist. That forces the order: record ghost 1 and ghost 2 first (either
# order), and only then record ghost 3. This ordering constraint — not maze complexity — is the
# puzzle. All branches are single-width dead ends off one straight spine, fully connected back
# to spawn (5,0).
#
# Convoy note (full explanation in 3-01): ghosts reset to spawn every loop and spawn's only
# exit is Up, so each later-departing actor idles one extra tick per already-moving actor
# before its first step, keeping a constant 1-tile gap up the shared spine.
#
# Loop 0 (no ghosts yet): live player takes the farther of the two door-A plates, plate 2
#   (2,4 — 7 raw moves), departing immediately. Ticks 0-3: Up x4 to (5,4). Ticks 4-6: Left x3
#   to (2,4)=plate 2, arriving tick 6. Manual split.
#   Ghost_2's recording: Up,Up,Up,Up,Left,Left,Left (7 ticks).
# Loop 1 (Ghost_2 replaying): live player idles tick 0, then takes plate 1 (8,2 — 5 raw moves).
#   Tick 0: idle. Ticks 1-2: Up x2 to (5,2). Ticks 3-5: Right x3 to (8,2)=plate 1, arriving
#   tick 5. Manual split. Ghost_1's recording: Idle,Up,Up,Right,Right,Right (6 ticks).
#   Convoy check: Ghost_2 stays 1 tick/tile ahead the whole way up the shared spine — vacate-
#   legal, no contest. Door A's AND condition is now satisfied by these two ghosts: plate 1
#   held from tick 5 (open from resolve 6), plate 2 held from tick 6 (open from resolve 7) —
#   door A is open for every tick from resolve 7 onward, for the rest of this attempt.
# Loop 2 (Ghost_2, Ghost_1 replaying; door A already open): live player idles ticks 0-1, then
#   walks all the way to plate 3 (8,8 — 11 raw moves: 8 up the spine, through door A, then 3
#   right along the branch). Ticks 2-7: Up x6 to (5,6)=door A tile, arriving tick 7 — door A's
#   state for that move reflects dispatch through tick 6, which already includes both plates'
#   activation (see above), so it opens exactly in time and the player walks straight through,
#   no bump. Ticks 8-9: Up x2 more to (5,8). Ticks 10-12: Right x3 to (8,8)=plate 3, arriving
#   tick 12. Manual split.
#   Ghost_3's recording: Idle,Idle,Up,Up,Up,Up,Up,Up,Up,Up,Right,Right,Right (13 ticks).
# Loop 3 (final — all three ghosts replaying, live player unencumbered): live player idles
#   ticks 0-2, then walks the spine straight through both doors to the goal (11 raw moves, no
#   turns — the branches are side spurs the live player never enters).
#   Ticks 3-8: Up x6, reaching door A (5,6) on tick 8 — door A has been open since resolve 7
#   (see loop 2), so the player walks straight through with no bump.
#   Ticks 9-11: Up x3 more, reaching (5,9) on tick 11.
#   Tick 12: the 10th move attempts door B (5,10). Ghost_3 replays identically every loop, so
#   it activates plate 3 at tick 12 in this loop too — but door B's state for the player's
#   tick-12 move reflects dispatch only through tick 11, before that activation lands. The
#   player bumps and stays at (5,9). This is the documented case: a ghost's plate-press and
#   the live player's door-crossing landing on the same tick costs exactly one extra tick.
#   Tick 13: retrying the same move, door B's state now reflects dispatch through tick 12
#   (plate 3 held) — door open, player crosses into (5,10).
#   Tick 14: Up once more onto (5,11)=goal. WinCondition satisfied at tick 14.
# Totals: 4 loops used (3 ghost loops + 1 final) against PAR_LOOPS: 4; 14 ticks in the winning
# loop (including the one natural bump at door B) against PAR_TICKS: 17.
NAME: 3-03 Two Gates
WORLD: 3
LOOP_SECONDS: 20
MAX_TIMELINES: 3
PAR_LOOPS: 4
PAR_TICKS: 17
